-- js-callgraph.rofl — the CLAIMS the call-graph model makes about itself.
-- Rules derive; this file asserts, and every assertion here is answerable by
-- `npm run report` rather than by reading prose.
--
-- shape_because is keyed by callee SHAPE: `member_expression` HAS a rule and still
-- misses seven of its eight shapes, and only the shape shows the residue.
--   checked                                           — evidence, not claim.

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE FRONTIER, BY SHAPE. Every shape whose sites do not all resolve has a row
-- here; `shape_unexplained[audit]` is the gate that says so, and it must be
-- empty. `runtime_dependent` appears EXACTLY ONCE, and that is the point of
-- the taxonomy: a computed callee `o[k]()` with a non-literal k names a method
-- that does not exist until the program runs, and nothing else on this list
-- can borrow that excuse. `a.b.c()` is merely unfinished.
shape_because(js, s_computed_dynamic_key,  callgraph, runtime_dependent).

-- ours, and on the queue
shape_because(js, s_identifier,            callgraph, not_yet).
-- FOUR REASONS RETIRED 2026-09-04, AND THE AUDIT IS WHAT DEMANDED IT.
-- `s_member_on_ident`, `s_member_on_member`, `s_member_on_this` and
-- `s_optional_member` used to say `not_yet` and every site of all four now
-- resolves through `denotes` — so `shape_stale[audit]`, the check for an excuse
-- outliving its cause, named them by name the moment tier 4 landed. Deleting
-- them is not optimism: an excuse for a shape whose sites all resolve is
-- decoration, and if a corpus ever brings back a receiver the entries do not
-- reach — an import, a parameter, a host global — the residue returns and
-- `shape_unexplained[audit]` demands a reason again. The verdict follows the
-- corpus rather than the grammar HERE, which is the opposite of the shape
-- vocabulary above, and the two audits are what keep the difference honest.
-- the residue is an AMBIENT receiver: `bag['fixed']` where `bag` is a
-- `declare const` with no initialiser, so there is no value in the program to
-- find. REVERTED TO not_yet 2026-09-04: I had written out_of_scope, and scope
-- is the owner's word rather than mine. Reading a `.d.ts` is undone, not
-- excluded, until somebody with the authority says otherwise.
shape_because(js, s_computed_literal_key,  callgraph, not_yet).

-- `s_passes` IS TICKED AND STILL HAS RESIDUE, which is what this table is
-- for and `unknown_because` is not: the cell is modelled — `notNull!(n)`
-- resolves through the value layer — while `maybe!(n)` does not, because
-- `maybe` is an AMBIENT declaration with no value in the program. REVERTED TO
-- not_yet for the same reason as above: an ambient declaration's type is
-- readable in principle, so this is undone rather than excluded, and excluding
-- it is not my call. Note the division of labour the two tables have:
-- `shape_because` describes the RESIDUE OF A SHAPE and may stand beside a
-- tick; `unknown_because` describes an UNMODELLED CELL and may not.
shape_because(js, s_passes,              callgraph, not_yet).
-- RETIRED 2026-09-08, and `shape_stale[audit]` is what named it: every site of
-- `s_computed_template_key` resolves now, so the excuse outlived its cause. It
-- read `knowable at parse time, and absent from the fact vocabulary`, which was
-- true of the contract and not of the language.
-- RETIRED 2026-09-04 with the value core: `mkBox(n).peek()` resolves through
-- the callee's RETURN, so this shape has no residue left and `shape_stale`
-- said so. It was the cell the queue recorded as waiting on the dataflow layer.
-- RETIRED 2026-09-04: `super.m()` resolves — `super` inside a class method
-- denotes the SUPERCLASS, which is one relation over the class's `super_class`
-- child. `shape_stale[audit]` named this row the moment the rule landed, for
-- the fourth time this session.
-- RETIRED 2026-09-04, AND NEITHER NEEDED A RULE. `new C().m()` and
-- `({a(){}}).a()` were `not_yet` because the corpus had no site for them, and
-- whether the rules covered them was a guess. Two sites in shapes.ts turned the
-- guess into a row: both resolve through `may_be_node` exactly as predicted —
-- a fresh instance reaches its class, an object literal is its own value.
-- `shape_stale[audit]` then named both. The excuse was about the CORPUS and
-- read as if it were about the MODEL, which is the difference a site settles
-- and an argument cannot.
-- `"str".toUpperCase()` and the like: the receiver is a primitive and the
-- method lives on its PROTOTYPE. REVERTED TO not_yet 2026-09-04. I had matched
-- it to `s_member_on_array`'s out_of_scope, and that was overreach twice over:
-- scope is the owner's word, and `s_member_on_array` carries a verdict I did
-- not write and cannot confirm either. Whether this model ever describes
-- built-in prototypes is a real question with a real answer; `scope_unowned`
-- keeps it on the table until somebody with the authority answers it.
-- RE-TYPED 2026-09-08 WITH ITS TWIN. `f_two_reason_relations_over_one_fact_have_now_bitten_twice`
-- records that `shape_because` (the FRONTIER's reason) and `unknown_because`
-- (the MATRIX's reason for the same cell) are taken for one fact by every
-- reader and derive from each other nowhere. Both are moved here in the same
-- edit, on purpose, so the pair does not diverge a third time.
shape_because(js, s_member_on_literal,     callgraph, no_source_target).
-- TWO NEW RESIDUES from the 2026-09-04 split of the object-position catch-all,
-- and each is owned by a DIFFERENT item, which is why they are two rows and
-- not one bucket: `(await p).m()` waits on the control-form work and
-- `` `s`.m() `` answers through the String prototype.
-- `s_member_on_await` RETIRED 2026-09-04: `await` is transparent to the value
-- layer now, the site resolves, and a reason with no residue is what
-- `shape_stale[audit]` exists to catch.
shape_because(js, s_member_on_template,    callgraph, no_source_target).
-- `s_member_on_other` KEEPS ITS ROW: the frontier reason describes the
-- RESIDUE OF A SHAPE, and the catcher has a residue the moment anything lands in
-- it. `unresolved_shape[audit]` reads this relation, so removing the row would
-- make an occupied catcher silent exactly when it matters.
shape_because(js, s_member_on_other,       callgraph, not_yet).
-- RETIRED 2026-09-05 by measurement: `(new Maker())()` where the constructor
-- RETURNS a function resolves to that function, through the same wrapper rule.
-- The shape had carried `not_yet` since it was named and no site had ever been
-- built that could contradict it — the corpus had no constructor with a return.
-- MODELLED 2026-09-04 with a residue that is not ours. `super()` reaches the
-- nearest ancestor's explicit constructor — measured against V8, which reports
-- `Cask -> Barrel` through a constructor-less `Keg`, skipping the synthesised
-- frame — and the site that stays is one whose whole chain has no explicit
-- constructor at all. There is no node for the function it calls.
shape_because(js, s_super,                 callgraph, no_source_target).
-- MODELLED 2026-11-05: an `import(p)` is a call that module_expr or module_ref
-- gives a module, and its shape is `s_dynamic_import` whatever the parser calls
-- it. It has no function to reach: the target is a module, so the residue is the
-- one `super()` has, no source target.
shape_because(js, s_dynamic_import,        callgraph, no_source_target).

-- the receiver is a host literal — see array_expression above
shape_because(js, s_member_on_array,       callgraph, out_of_scope).

-- A shape nothing produced. It exists so that a grammar form nobody
-- anticipated is a ROW rather than a silence, and the day it appears the
-- verdict is already `not_yet` instead of nothing at all.
shape_because(js, s_unclassified,          callgraph, not_yet).

-- TWO SHAPES GAINED A RESIDUE ON 2026-09-05, and the residue is the fix rather
-- than a regression. `Vat.poured()` and `new Vat().tapped()` are TypeErrors —
-- an instance method reached through the class, a static reached through an
-- instance — and until the receiver decided which half of a class it can see,
-- both RESOLVED. They now resolve to nothing, which puts one unresolved site
-- into each of these two shapes and `shape_unexplained[audit]` demanded a
-- reason for both.
--
-- THE REASON IS THE ONE ALREADY IN THE TAXONOMY and it fits exactly: the same
-- atom `super()` earned when its whole chain declares no constructor. There is
-- no node for the function these sites name, because the program names a
-- function that does not exist. `not_yet` would be a queue entry nobody can
-- ever discharge, which is the mistake the call-graph sweep spent a day
-- correcting one level up.
shape_because(js, s_member_on_ident, callgraph, no_source_target).
shape_because(js, s_member_on_new,   callgraph, no_source_target).

-- A NINTH RECEIVER SHAPE, 2026-09-08 (w_meta_property). `import.meta.resolve()`
-- is a call into the HOST, and the residue is irreducible for a reason that is
-- in TypeScript's own files rather than in an argument: `interface ImportMeta`
-- in `lib.es5.d.ts` is EMPTY, and `url`/`resolve` are declared in
-- `lib.dom.d.ts` and `@types/node`. `releaseOf` in scanners/ts_lib.ts returns
-- null for both — no release in the file name — so `lib_member` cannot carry
-- them and `lib_unsupported[audit]` could never date them. This is the first
-- residue on this list whose callee is the HOST's rather than the LANGUAGE's,
-- and the distinction is exactly the one the era axis cannot represent.
shape_because(js, s_member_on_meta,  callgraph, no_source_target).

-- DECLARED HERE AND ABSENT FROM THE CORPUS, with the reason written down;
-- `kind_unexercised[audit]` demands one, and a row belongs where its kind is
-- declared.
-- `decorator`'s EXCUSE WENT ON 2026-09-08: the corpus has two, in
-- test/fixtures/js-call/shapes.ts.txt, and `kind_absent_stale[audit]` named the
-- row within one run of the fixture landing.
-- ...AND `class_accessor_property` NEVER NEEDED ONE. The first draft wrote it
-- an excuse in the same edit that gave it a site, and `kind_absent_stale[audit]`
-- reported the row within one run — an excuse and its cause landing together
-- is the same defect as an excuse outliving its cause, caught by the same rule.
-- `tagged_template_expression` stood here until 2026-09-07 and the corpus grew
-- three sites — two runnable and one in shapes.ts that exists so the `fn_node`
-- guard has something to bite on. `kind_absent_stale[audit]` is what would have
-- said so if the author had not.
